    "message": "feat(routing): let a route's primary model reorder with its fallbacks\n\nEvery fallback on the routing page had up/down arrows; the primary model\nabove them did not. But a route is one ordered path -- MODEL plus the\ncomma-joined chain beside it -- so the arrows stopped exactly at the entry\nthat actually serves the traffic. Promoting a fallback meant retyping two\nfields by hand and hoping they agreed.\n\nThe rail now reorders as the single list it looks like. The primary gets the\nsame two buttons every row under it has, wired to the ModelChainEditor next\nto it, which is what owns the ordering rules. All six rails share one builder\n(appendRouteRail) -- the four tier overrides, the default route and the\nvision adapter -- because a rail that reorders differently from the one\nbeside it reads as a bug, not as a distinction.\n\nOnly a SWAP is offered across the boundary, never an insert, so no button\npress can leave a route without a primary. Both halves of that matter and\nthey fail differently: an empty MODEL fails validation, so the server\nrefuses to start; an empty tier override is quiet and worse, because routing\nreads a route's own fallbacks only when that route has its own primary, so\nclearing MODEL_OPUS silently orphans MODEL_OPUS_FALLBACKS and the tier drops\nback to the root chain with nothing in the log to say so. Demotion is\ntherefore gated on there being a fallback to swap with, and re-gates live as\nthe primary is typed in. Promotion deliberately is not gated that way:\npromoting into an unset override is exactly how a route stops inheriting the\ndefault, and there the row is removed rather than left holding an empty value.\n\nVerification\n------------\nDriven in jsdom against a real running server (throwaway HOME, port 8147),\n29 assertions, all passing -- including a demote/promote round trip that\nleaves both hidden inputs byte-identical to where they started, and\nchangedValues() carrying both settings so Apply writes them together.\n\nPositive control, same harness against unmodified main:\n\n    port 8148 (main)   rails: 6   primary move clusters: 0\n    port 8147 (branch) rails: 6   primary move clusters: 6\n\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing by name:\n\n    M1 a bespoke rail built outside appendRouteRail  -> reorders_a_route_rail\n    M2 setPrimary wiring dropped (dead arrows)       -> reorders_a_route_rail\n    M3 demote gate relaxed to allow an empty primary -> never_lets_a_button_empty\n    M4 promote gate tightened (unset stays unset)    -> never_lets_a_button_empty\n    M5 down button stops honouring the gate          -> never_lets_a_button_empty\n    M6 the new CSS block lost in a merge             -> styles_every_class\n    M7 routing merges an orphaned chain              -> orphaned_when_its_override\n\nThe orphan behaviour the UI rule is built around is now pinned in\ntest_routing_chains.py rather than left as a comment, so nobody \"fixes\" the\nbutton gate into a bug.\n\nLocal CI: ruff format --check, ruff check and ty all clean; pytest\n4795 passed, 26 skipped, with the documented 6 test_install_ps1_* checksum\nfailures that are the standing local baseline on this machine.\n\nNot done\n--------\nThe \"Custom route\" / \"Inherits default\" chip on a tier card is computed at\nrender and does not update when a promotion fills a previously unset\noverride. Pre-existing -- typing in the field does the same -- and out of\nscope here.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
